"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative findings included a high preoperative PSA of 190 and a biopsy demonstrating a Gleason 4+3 pattern with perineural invasion. The surgical findings indicate a pT3b stage, positive surgical margins, seminal vesicle invasion, lymphovascular invasion, and lymph node metastasis. The positive nodal status is a significant adverse factor.\n\nCountervailing findings are less documented, as previous notes were unavailable, which widens uncertainty regarding the overall clinical course. The discrepancy between the biopsy grade (ISUP grade group 3) and the prostatectomy grade (ISUP grade group 2) also contributes to prognostic variability. Missing postoperative PSA measurements and subsequent trajectory information are critical unknowns that would substantially revise the prognosis. The current horizon suggests a potential event within the calculated range. The fixed estimated horizon is 27.33 months. This is a predicted recurrence horizon, not evidence of observed recurrence. The sensitivity range is 20.49–34.16 months; its coverage has not been validated."
